Determine benchmark scope, distribution, and target profile (1–2 days) — Confirm which Kubernetes distribution is in play (self-managed, EKS, AKS, GKE, OpenShift, etc.) and its version, because the benchmark itself splits into distribution-specific editions: CIS Kubernetes Benchmark applies to the open-source distribution and aims to be universally applicable, while CIS releases separate EKS/AKS/GKE benchmarks with guidelines specific to the components you actually control in each managed offering. Pull the exact matching edition and version (the general benchmark alone runs well over 250 pages of recommendations). Decide whether the org is targeting Level 1 or Level 2: Level 1 recommends essential basic security requirements that can be configured on any system and should cause little or no interruption of service or reduced functionality, while Level 2 recommends stricter settings for higher-security environments that could result in some reduced functionality. Deliverable: a one-page scope note naming the benchmark edition, version, and profile level to assess against. 
Microsoft Learn
Select, configure, and validate assessment tooling (1–2 days) — Research and compare the realistic tooling options: kube-bench (CLI-based, audits API server flags, kubelet configuration, file permissions and etcd config directly), Kubescape, and Trivy's built-in Kubernetes compliance mode, which runs named compliance specs like k8s-cis-1.23 mapping each CIS control ID to concrete automated checks. Critically, confirm the tool's benchmark version is pinned to match what was decided in ticket 1 — third-party scanners often lag: one platform team explicitly pins a cis-1.11 kube-bench config to target Kubernetes 1.29–1.32, and GKE's own docs warn that the versions of the CIS Benchmarks these applications evaluate might not be the latest available. Run a first scan against a non-production cluster to sanity-check output before it's trusted for real findings. Deliverable: working scanner producing a raw pass/fail/warn report.
Assess Control Plane Components and etcd (Sections 1–2) (1–2 days, mark N/A if fully managed) — Audit API server, controller manager, scheduler, and etcd configuration flags (TLS enforcement, client-cert-auth, encryption at rest). On managed distributions this is largely not directly actionable — for AKS specifically, recommendations may resolve to "N/A" because the cloud provider deploys control-plane pods via its own mechanism rather than node-VM manifest files the check expects, or "Equivalent Control," where the recommendation was implemented in a different, equivalent manner by the provider. Manually spot-check anything the scanner marks FAIL or WARN rather than trusting the automated verdict outright. Deliverable: section findings with each control marked Compliant/Partial/Missing/N/A and a one-line justification.
Assess Control Plane Configuration and any cloud-specific overlay (Section 3 + managed-benchmark items) (1–2 days) — Cover audit logging and admission controller/webhook configuration. If running a managed distribution, run the corresponding cloud-specific CIS benchmark in parallel for the items the generic benchmark can't reach — for EKS alone this spans control plane configuration (audit logging, endpoint security), RBAC and service-account security, pod security standards, and managed-service items like ECR and networking/encryption, roughly 46+ recommendations across five areas in that benchmark version alone. Cross-reference the provider's own CIS mapping documentation for which items they already satisfy on your behalf. Deliverable: combined findings sheet for this section plus the cloud overlay.
Assess Worker Node / kubelet configuration (Section 4) (1–2 days) — This section stays fully actionable even on managed clusters, since node-level kubelet settings remain the customer's responsibility. Check kubelet config file permissions, anonymous-auth, client CA settings, and authorization mode across every node pool/instance type in use (not just one representative node — flag any drift between pools). Deliverable: per-node-pool findings, since results can legitimately differ across pools.
Assess RBAC (Section 5.1) (1 day) — Review every ClusterRole and Role for wildcard usage, since wildcards grant broad permissions that may exceed what is actually required, and the standard remediation is to replace wildcards with explicit resource names, API groups, and verbs. Also check for unnecessary direct pod-create permissions, since direct pod creation bypasses higher-level controllers like Deployments that provide replica management and rolling updates. Include service accounts, not just human-facing roles. Deliverable: a prioritized list of over-permissive bindings, ranked by blast radius (cluster-admin-equivalent first).
Assess Pod Security Standards (Section 5.2) (1 day) — Check whether the built-in Restricted/Baseline/Privileged profiles are enforced (these replaced the deprecated PodSecurityPolicy from Kubernetes 1.25 onward), and where they're only partially enforced, identify which namespaces are still running privileged containers, containers as root, or containers with access to host namespaces/filesystem. Deliverable: namespace-by-namespace enforcement matrix.
Assess Network Policies (Section 5.3) (1 day) — Kubernetes networking is open by default with no segmentation between pods until policies are added, so check for a default-deny baseline per namespace with explicit allow rules layered on top — the standard hardening pattern for reducing lateral movement. Test that policies actually apply as intended (a NetworkPolicy resource existing isn't the same as traffic actually being blocked) rather than just checking for its presence. Deliverable: namespace coverage map plus a note on any policies confirmed present but not effective.
Assess Secrets management and remaining policy items (Section 5.4+) (1 day) — Confirm whether Secrets are encrypted at rest (by default Kubernetes only base64-encodes them, which is not encryption), and check seccomp profiles, AppArmor/SELinux where applicable, and sysctl restrictions. Deliverable: findings on encryption-at-rest status plus remaining minor policy gaps.
Consolidate the gap register and run impact/effort prioritization (1–2 days) — Merge outputs from tickets 3–9 into one register: each control tagged Compliant/Partial/Missing/N/A, its Level (1 or 2), and whether it's automated or requires manual verification. Run a short prioritization session with the platform team to score each gap on impact and remediation effort, separating genuine quick wins from items needing a design discussion (e.g., a cluster-wide network policy rollout is not a quick win even if scored high-impact). Deliverable: ranked gap register that ticket 11's scope gets pulled from directly.
Remediate the agreed set of priority gaps (2–3 days, scope pulled from ticket 10's output) — Implement the highest-ranked fixes from the register, validating each in non-production before promoting. Treat this as a batch covering however many quick-win items the team agreed to take on this cycle rather than one fix per gap; anything requiring broader design work (e.g., org-wide RBAC rework, full network policy rollout) gets flagged for its own follow-up ticket instead of being squeezed in here. Deliverable: fixed items re-scanned and confirmed passing, plus a short list of what was deliberately deferred and why.
Write the findings/recommendations report and groom the remaining backlog (1–2 days) — Produce the write-up: current compliance posture by section, what was remediated this cycle, and a prioritized roadmap for everything still open (including anything deferred out of ticket 11 or marked N/A for later re-evaluation, e.g. if the platform migrates distributions). Turn every deferred item into its own tracked backlog ticket so nothing gets lost. Deliverable: the report itself plus a populated backlog for the next cycle.

That's roughly 12 tickets at a day or two each, so figure 15–20 working days end to end depending on how many quick wins ticket 11 ends up covering. Want this as a doc, or should I adjust the grouping further?

